<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:32:default:gcc">
<!--
    kuna calleepreserves: an i386 get-PC thunk eats a live argument.

    x86gcc.cspec lists ECX and EDX as killedbycall because cdecl allows a callee
    to clobber them, and Heritage::guardCalls (heritage.cc:1444) applies that
    answer to every call.  It is an answer about the CONVENTION, and at a get-PC
    thunk it is false: gcc emits "mov ebx,[esp]; ret", four bytes that write EBX
    and nothing else, once per PIE that reaches a global, precisely because it is
    cheaper than a convention-abiding call.

    The bytes are function sub_8049f20 of the i386 PIE "collide", verbatim, with
    its thunk at 0x8049f55 immediately after it:

        8049f20  55                 PUSH   EBP
        8049f21  89 e5              MOV    EBP,ESP
        8049f23  83 ec 10           SUB    ESP,0x10
        8049f26  89 5d fc           MOV    [EBP + -0x4],EBX
        8049f29  8b 55 0c           MOV    EDX,[EBP + 0xc]   ; the second parameter
        8049f2c  e8 24 00 00 00     CALL   0x8049f55         ; the get-PC thunk
        8049f31  81 c3 6f 01 00 00  ADD    EBX,0x16f
        8049f37  c7 04 24 03 ...    MOV    [ESP],0x3
        8049f3e  89 54 24 08        MOV    [ESP + 0x8],EDX   ; third argument of the call
        8049f42  8b 55 08           MOV    EDX,[EBP + 0x8]   ; the first parameter
        8049f45  89 54 24 04        MOV    [ESP + 0x4],EDX
        8049f49  e8 12 e4 ff ff     CALL   0x8048360
        8049f4e  8b 5d fc           MOV    EBX,[EBP + -0x4]
        8049f51  89 ec              MOV    ESP,EBP
        8049f53  5d                 POP    EBP
        8049f54  c3                 RET
        8049f55  8b 1c 24           MOV    EBX,[ESP]
        8049f58  c3                 RET

    The controlled comparison is inside the one function: the first parameter is
    loaded from the frame AFTER the call and is recovered, the second is loaded
    BEFORE it and is lost.  The only difference between them is that EDX crosses
    the CALL.

    0x8048360 stands in for the PLT entry the original called and is
    "xor eax,eax; ret".  Its body writes EAX (an output and likelytrash register)
    and ESP, and no register x86gcc.cspec marks unaffected, so it never departs
    from the convention and its own killed set is left exactly as written; both
    passes render that call identically.

    Stage: P3 heritage call guarding, option calleepreserves off|on.  Pass 1
    (off) pins the bug, pass 2 (on, the default) pins the fix.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x8049f20">
5589e583ec10895dfc8b550ce82400000081c36f010000c7042403000000895424088b550889542404e812e4ffff8b5dfc89ec5dc38b1c24c3
</bytechunk>
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x8048360">
31c0c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x8049f20" name="f"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x8048360" name="reportstat"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>option calleepreserves off</com>
  <com>lo fu f</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option calleepreserves on</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="CALLEEPRESERVES #1: off, the value that crossed the thunk is a local nothing assigns" min="1" max="1">unsigned int v1; // edx</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="CALLEEPRESERVES #2: off, f loses the parameter that value was loaded from" min="1" max="1">void f\(unsigned int a0\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="CALLEEPRESERVES #3: on, EDX survives the thunk and f regains its second parameter" min="1" max="1">void f\(unsigned int a0,unsigned int a1\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="CALLEEPRESERVES #4: on, the third argument is the caller's own parameter, not an undefined local" min="1" max="1">v1 = a1;</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="CALLEEPRESERVES #5: both passes, the second call is rendered identically (its callee writes no unaffected register)" min="2" max="2">reportstat\(3,a0,v1\);</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="CALLEEPRESERVES #6: both passes, the thunk is still called and still returns nothing" min="2" max="2">sub_8049f55\(\);</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
